1
00:00:00,450 --> 00:00:04,274
At the clinic, a nurse clips a
small device onto Diya's finger.

2
00:00:04,274 --> 00:00:07,483
In a few seconds, two
numbers appear on its screen.

3
00:00:07,833 --> 00:00:09,580
How can light read your blood?

4
00:00:10,080 --> 00:00:14,349
Medical electronic instruments
measure signals from the human body.

5
00:00:14,699 --> 00:00:17,733
Almost every one is a
chain of three blocks.

6
00:00:17,733 --> 00:00:22,432
First, a sensor called a transducer
turns a body signal into an electrical

7
00:00:22,432 --> 00:00:23,332
signal.

8
00:00:23,451 --> 00:00:26,402
Then an amplifier makes
that tiny signal bigger.

9
00:00:26,402 --> 00:00:29,862
And a display shows the
result as a number or a graph.

10
00:00:30,512 --> 00:00:32,220
Let's meet the common ones.

11
00:00:32,570 --> 00:00:37,239
A digital thermometer uses a thermistor,
a part whose resistance changes

12
00:00:37,239 --> 00:00:38,530
with temperature.

13
00:00:38,530 --> 00:00:42,648
Normal body temperature is about
thirty seven degrees Celsius.

14
00:00:42,998 --> 00:00:47,376
A sphygmomanometer measures blood
pressure in the arteries, in millimetres

15
00:00:47,376 --> 00:00:48,281
of mercury.

16
00:00:48,281 --> 00:00:51,883
For a healthy adult it is
about one twenty over eighty.

17
00:00:52,233 --> 00:00:56,485
A pulse oximeter shines red light and
infrared light through the finger.

18
00:00:56,485 --> 00:01:00,366
A light sensor on the other side
measures how much gets through.

19
00:01:00,716 --> 00:01:03,697
Blood rich in oxygen
absorbs more infrared.

20
00:01:03,697 --> 00:01:06,519
Blood with less oxygen absorbs more red.

21
00:01:06,519 --> 00:01:11,255
Comparing the two gives the oxygen
level, and the pulses give the heart

22
00:01:11,255 --> 00:01:12,155
rate.

23
00:01:12,197 --> 00:01:16,099
An ECG records the electrical
signals of the heart.

24
00:01:16,099 --> 00:01:20,791
An EEG records the much weaker
electrical signals of the brain.

25
00:01:21,441 --> 00:01:23,949
Let's build the ECG on the breadboard.

26
00:01:24,299 --> 00:01:27,378
Electrodes on the skin
pick up the heart's signal.

27
00:01:27,378 --> 00:01:29,868
It is tiny, only about one millivolt.

28
00:01:30,218 --> 00:01:35,083
An amplifier with a gain of one thousand
makes it about one volt, big enough

29
00:01:35,083 --> 00:01:36,006
to display.

30
00:01:36,356 --> 00:01:37,562
Watch the scope.

31
00:01:37,562 --> 00:01:39,776
Channel one shows the tiny input.

32
00:01:39,776 --> 00:01:43,549
Channel two shows the same
shape, a thousand times bigger.

33
00:01:43,899 --> 00:01:46,171
Each beat draws the same pattern.

34
00:01:46,171 --> 00:01:51,067
A small P wave, a tall spike called
the QRS complex, and a T wave.

35
00:01:51,417 --> 00:01:54,921
The P wave comes from the
upper chambers, the atria.

36
00:01:54,921 --> 00:01:59,748
The QRS complex comes from the lower
chambers, the ventricles, just before

37
00:01:59,748 --> 00:02:00,754
they squeeze.

38
00:02:00,754 --> 00:02:04,002
The T wave shows the
ventricles returning to rest.

39
00:02:04,652 --> 00:02:06,727
Sensor, amplifier, display.

40
00:02:06,727 --> 00:02:09,034
That is every medical instrument.

41
00:02:09,834 --> 00:02:12,555
So how does the machine
count your heart rate?

42
00:02:12,555 --> 00:02:14,598
From the gap between two R peaks.

43
00:02:14,948 --> 00:02:19,437
The time between two R peaks is
called the RR interval, in seconds.

44
00:02:20,137 --> 00:02:22,245
One beat takes that much time.

45
00:02:22,245 --> 00:02:25,601
So in one second, there
are one over RR beats.

46
00:02:26,301 --> 00:02:28,138
A minute has sixty seconds.

47
00:02:28,138 --> 00:02:33,078
So the heart rate is sixty divided by
the RR interval, in beats per minute.

48
00:02:33,778 --> 00:02:38,669
And the gain of the amplifier is the
output voltage divided by the input

49
00:02:38,669 --> 00:02:39,569
voltage.

50
00:02:39,443 --> 00:02:40,794
It has no unit.

51
00:02:41,444 --> 00:02:42,568
Worked example.

52
00:02:42,568 --> 00:02:47,585
On the scope, two R peaks are four
divisions apart, at zero point two seconds

53
00:02:47,585 --> 00:02:48,609
per division.

54
00:02:48,959 --> 00:02:52,188
The RR interval is four
times zero point two.

55
00:02:52,188 --> 00:02:54,505
That is zero point eight seconds.

56
00:02:55,105 --> 00:02:58,521
The heart rate is sixty
divided by zero point eight.

57
00:02:58,521 --> 00:03:00,552
Seventy five beats per minute.

58
00:03:01,152 --> 00:03:04,805
And the gain is one volt
divided by one millivolt.

59
00:03:04,805 --> 00:03:05,917
One thousand.

60
00:03:06,567 --> 00:03:08,033
Now, the exam trap.

61
00:03:08,383 --> 00:03:11,953
Many students think an ECG
measures blood pressure.

62
00:03:11,953 --> 00:03:13,004
It does not.

63
00:03:13,354 --> 00:03:16,688
An ECG records the heart's
electrical signals.

64
00:03:16,688 --> 00:03:21,795
Blood pressure is measured by a
sphygmomanometer, in millimetres of mercury.

65
00:03:22,445 --> 00:03:23,897
Where is this asked?

66
00:03:23,897 --> 00:03:25,350
In PUC board papers.

67
00:03:25,350 --> 00:03:29,873
Name the instrument for a quantity,
give its use, or draw the blocks of

68
00:03:29,873 --> 00:03:31,316
a medical instrument.

69
00:03:31,966 --> 00:03:32,866
Try this.

70
00:03:32,837 --> 00:03:37,618
An ECG is recorded on paper that
moves at twenty five millimetres per

71
00:03:37,618 --> 00:03:38,518
second.

72
00:03:38,319 --> 00:03:41,262
Two R peaks are fifteen millimetres apart.

73
00:03:41,262 --> 00:03:43,068
What is the heart rate?

74
00:03:48,668 --> 00:03:49,568
C.

75
00:03:49,063 --> 00:03:50,988
One hundred beats per minute.

76
00:03:51,338 --> 00:03:56,040
The RR interval is fifteen millimetres
divided by twenty five millimetres

77
00:03:56,040 --> 00:03:57,007
per second.

78
00:03:57,358 --> 00:03:59,415
That is zero point six seconds.

79
00:03:59,765 --> 00:04:03,101
The heart rate is sixty
divided by zero point six.

80
00:04:03,101 --> 00:04:05,093
One hundred beats per minute.

81
00:04:05,743 --> 00:04:07,264
Screenshot this card.

82
00:04:10,464 --> 00:04:12,903
Next, fixed and variable resistors.

83
00:04:12,903 --> 00:04:16,144
How does turning a volume
knob change the sound?
